'''Jeanna Giese-Frassetto'''<ref name="Razner">{{cite news |last1=Razner |first1=Sarah |title=15 years after she survived rabies, Jeanna Giese seeks to save others from it |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-reporter-fondy-bat-bite-sept-22-2019/177140211/ |access-date=June 9, 2026 |work=[[USA Today]] |agency=[[The Reporter (Fond du Lac, Wisconsin)|The Reporter]] |date=September 22, 2019 |page=A1}}</ref> (born June 6, 1989) is an American [[rabies]] survivor. In 2004, when she was aged 15, she was bitten by a bat. A month later in October, she developed flu-symptoms and her physical state quickly deteriorated. After she was diagnosed with rabies, doctors at [[Children's Wisconsin]] used an experimental treatment called the [[Milwaukee protocol]] to put her in an [[induced coma]] to allow for her body to produce the antibodies needed to fend off the virus. She awoke from her coma a week later and became the first known survivor of rabies who did not take the [[rabies vaccine]].

== Early life and education ==
Jeanna Giese was born to parents Ann and John Giese on June 6, 1989, in [[Fond du Lac, Wisconsin]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Births – Fond du Lac, St. Agnes Hospital |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-oshkosh-northwestern-births-fond-du/199281579/ |access-date=June 10, 2026 |work=[[Oshkosh Northwestern]] |date=June 19, 1989 |page=5}}</ref> She has three brothers<ref>{{cite news |last1=Russell |first1=Renee DuFore |title=Jeanna Giese returns home to Fond du Lac |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-oshkosh-northwestern-jeanna-giese-re/199289978/ |access-date=June 10, 2026 |work=[[Oshkosh Northwestern]] |date=January 3, 2005 |page=C3}}</ref> and was raised [[Catholic]].<ref name="v036" /> Giese attended [[St. Mary's Springs Academy]],<ref name="v036">{{cite book |last=Giese |first=Jeanna |title=History of Rabies in the Americas, Volume III |chapter=Surviving Rabies |publisher=Springer Nature Switzerland |publication-place=Cham |date=2025 |isbn=978-3-031-85084-4 |doi=10.1007/978-3-031-85085-1_13 |url=https://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-3-031-85085-1_13 |access-date=June 9, 2026 |pages=299–304}}</ref>{{rp|299}} where she played volleyball, and graduated in 2007.<ref name="d764" />{{rp|91, 95}}

== Rabies ==
On September 12, 2004,<ref name="r230" /><ref name="Rosenthal" /> 15-year-old Giese was attending church in Fond du Lac when a bat entered.<ref name="d764">{{cite journal |last=Willoughby |first=Rodney E. |title=A CURE for RABIES? |journal=Scientific American |publisher=Scientific American, a division of Nature America, Inc. |volume=296 |issue=4 |year=2007 |issn=0036-8733 |jstor=26069234 |pages=88–95 |url=http://www.jstor.org/stable/26069234 |access-date=June 9, 2026}}</ref>{{rp|89–90}} Giese picked up the bat to take it outside, and it bit her left hand's [[index finger]].<ref name="d764" />{{rp|90}} Giese treated the injury with [[hydrogen peroxide]] at home but did not otherwise seek medical attention, such as [[immunization]], which is recommended after contact with bats.<ref name="d764" />{{rp|90–91}}

For a month, the [[rabies virus]] duplicated and began traveling towards her brain through a [[nerve]], at approximately one centimeter per hour.<ref name="d764" />{{rp|91}} A month after the bite, on October 13, 2004,<ref name="r230" /> Giese developed [[flu]] symptoms. Her bitten hand became numb, she experienced [[double vision]], and had weakness of her left leg.<ref name="d764" />{{rp|91}} She was admitted to a local hospital two days later,<ref name="r230" /> where doctors assumed she had [[autoimmune encephalitis]] after imaging showed her brain to be normal.<ref name="d764" />{{rp|91}} She was discharged due to the fact,<ref name="d944" />{{rp|31}} and went to the [[Children's Wisconsin]] on October 18 after her illness worsened.<ref name="d764" />{{rp|91}}<ref name="Rosenthal" /><ref name="d944" />{{rp|31}} Her temperature went up to {{convert|102|F|C}}, her speech became slurred, she had an elevated [[white blood cell]] count, and she experienced tremors in her bitten arm, amongst other symptoms.<ref name="d944" />{{rp|31}}

Doctors at the hospital heard about the bat and realized she had rabies. They sent Giese's saliva, spinal fluid, skin and blood to be sampled at the rabies laboratory in the [[Centers for Disease Control and Prevention]] (CDC), which took 24 hours.<ref name="d764" />{{rp|91}} At this point, Giese was [[lethargic]], weak, and unbalanced, although she could still perform basic instructions. While waiting for the results, Rodney E. Willoughby Jr., who was attending to Jeanna, contacted [[Cathleen Hanlon]], a rabies specialist at the CDC, who informed him that it was unlikely Giese could be saved.<ref name="d764" />{{rp|92}} Once a rabies victim displays symptoms, they usually die within a few days.<ref name="p568">{{cite journal |last=Seppa |first=Nathan |title=One in a Million |journal=Science News |volume=167 |issue=5 |date=January 29, 2005 |doi=10.2307/4016069 |page=77 |url=http://doi.wiley.com/10.2307/4016069 |access-date=June 9, 2026|url-access=subscription }}</ref>

Through Willoughby's research, he discovered that rabies victims who died after multiple weeks often had no trace of the virus in their bodies and their brains were normal, indicating that the body could eradicate the virus, although it was too slow to do so. With this information, he theorized that by putting Giese in an [[induced coma]], the disease could be eradicated from her body.<ref name="d764" />{{rp|92}}

Giese's parents were informed of her diagnosis, and were made aware that she would likely die. They consented to the experiment in the hopes that it could aid in rabies research.<ref name="d764" />{{rp|93}} On October 19,<ref>{{cite news |last1=Johnson |first1=Mark |title=Woman who made rabies history graduates from college |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/detroit-free-press-woman-who-made-rabies/199248175/ |access-date=June 9, 2026 |work=[[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]] |date=June 6, 2011 |page=23}}</ref> the team at Children's Hospital used [[ketamine]] to induce the coma, supported by [[amantadine]], [[midazolam]], and [[phenobarbital]]. She was kept in the coma for a week, where the team observed no typical symptoms of late-stage rabies such as rapid heart rate and blood pressure.<ref name="d764" />{{rp|93}} At the end of the week, her body began producing [[antibodies]] to prevent the virus from attacking new cells. When the team brought her back from the coma, she was [[paralyzed]]. However, a day later, she gained movement in her legs and tried opening her eyes.<ref name="d764" />{{rp|94}} Giese said "I was basically a newborn baby at the age of 15. I couldn't do anything".<ref name="Elliott">{{cite news |last1=Elliott |first1=Tim |title=20 years later, Fond du Lac woman reflects on being 1st to survive rabies without vaccine |url=https://fox11online.com/news/local/20-years-later-fond-du-lac-woman-reflects-on-being-1st-to-survive-rabies-without-vaccine-jeanna-giese-bat-centers-disease-control-willoughby-coma-medical-marvel |access-date=June 10, 2026 |work=[[WLUK-TV]] |date=May 10, 2024 |archive-date=June 9, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260609014937/https://fox11online.com/news/local/20-years-later-fond-du-lac-woman-reflects-on-being-1st-to-survive-rabies-without-vaccine-jeanna-giese-bat-centers-disease-control-willoughby-coma-medical-marvel |url-status=live }}</ref>

As a result of her coma, Giese lost all strength, stamina, and coordination, and had a long recovery period. She could sit up on the bed by the 12th day after being brought back from the coma.<ref name="d764" />{{rp|94}} She was diagnosed with [[biopterin]] deficiency, which is a deficiency of the molecule responsible for some cell growth and neurotransmitters such as [[dopamine]], [[epinephrine]], [[norepinephrine]], [[serotonin]], and [[melatonin]].<ref name="d764" />{{rp|94–95}} After she was supplemented with biopterin, Giese could speak and swallow.<ref name="d764" />{{rp|95}} Giese became the first known person to survive rabies without receiving the [[rabies vaccine]],<ref name="o152">{{cite journal |last1=Yam |first1=Philip |last2=Choi |first2=Charles Q. |title=UPDATES |journal=Scientific American |publisher=Scientific American, a division of Nature America, Inc. |volume=300 |issue=2 |year=2009 |issn=0036-8733 |jstor=26001201 |pages=16–17 |url=http://www.jstor.org/stable/26001201 |access-date=June 9, 2026}}</ref>{{rp|16}}<ref name="r230">{{cite news |title=Rabies survivor goes home |work=[[Daily Journal (Illinois)|Kankakee Daily Journal]] |date=January 2, 2005 |jstor=community.39522409 |url=https://jstor.org/stable/community.39522409 |access-date=June 9, 2026 |page=A7}}</ref><ref name="Rosenthal">{{cite news |last1=Rosenthal |first1=Elisabeth |title=Girl Is First to Survive Rabies Without a Shot |url=https://go.gale.com/ps/i.do?p=AONE&u=anon~5f5c5f37&id=GALE%7CA125280854&v=2.1&it=r&sid=googleScholar&asid=0dc6a93d |access-date=June 9, 2026 |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=November 25, 2004}}</ref> and became famous.<ref name="v036" /> She also became the second person to survive rabies after a bat bite; the first survivor was vaccinated.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Johnson |first1=Mark |last2=Newton |first2=Kawanza |title=World's first rabies survivor |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/edmonton-journal-worlds-first-rabies-su/199289916/ |access-date=June 10, 2026 |work=[[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]] |agency=[[Edmonton Journal]] |date=December 11, 2005 |page=E4}}</ref> She did not suffer impact to her mental and cognitive abilities from the rabies or coma.<ref name="Hellpap" />

By November 30, a fundraiser for Giese's family had raised $47,000.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Russell |first1=Renee DuFore |title=Benefit for Jeanna raises $47,000 |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-reporter-fondy-bat-bite-nov-30-2004/167587595/ |access-date=June 9, 2026 |work=[[The Reporter (Fond du Lac, Wisconsin)|The Reporter]] |date=November 30, 2004 |page=1}}</ref> She left the hospital on January 1, 2005, with her family.<ref name="d764" />{{rp|95}}<ref name="d944">{{cite journal |last=Goldrick |first=Barbara A. |title=Emerging Infections: Human Rabies |journal=The American Journal of Nursing |publisher=Lippincott Williams & Wilkins |volume=105 |issue=5 |year=2005 |issn=0002-936X |jstor=29745727 |pages=31–34 |url=http://www.jstor.org/stable/29745727 |access-date=June 9, 2026}}</ref>{{rp|31}} Giese's care cost $800,000 ({{Inflation|US|800000|2004|fmt=eq}}).<ref name="d764" />{{rp|95}} The treatment used to save Giese was named the [[Milwaukee protocol]].<ref name="d764" />{{rp|95}}<ref name="n139" />{{rp|28}}

Although the Milwaukee protocol was attempted 6 times since Giese's success as of 2007, they all resulted in deaths. This has led to queries about how Giese did not die. Some rabies experts suggested that Giese was infected with a weak variant of rabies. As the CDC could not collect samples of the virus itself, this theory can neither be proven nor disproven.<ref name="d764" />{{rp|95}} As of 2015, there were 31 failures of the method, and critics highlighted issues with its reliability for human rabies patients.<ref name="j805">{{cite journal |last1=Zeiler |first1=Frederick A. |last2=Jackson |first2=Alan C. |title=Critical Appraisal of the Milwaukee Protocol for Rabies: This Failed Approach Should Be Abandoned |journal=[[Canadian Journal of Neurological Sciences]] |volume=43 |issue=1 |date=2015 |issn=0317-1671 |doi=10.1017/cjn.2015.331 |pages=44–51 |url=https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S0317167115003315/type/journal_article |access-date=June 10, 2026}}</ref>{{rp|44}} The same year, specialists reached the conclusion that the protocol was ineffective.<ref>{{cite magazine |last1=Lite |first1=Jordan |title=How Did Someone Survive Rabies without a Vaccine? |url=https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/jeanna-giese-rabies-survivor/ |magazine=[[Scientific American]] |access-date=June 10, 2026 |date=October 8, 2008 |archive-date=December 10, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131210131741/http://www.scientificamerican.com/article.cfm?id=jeanna-giese-rabies-survivor |url-status=live }}</ref>

Giese is the most well-known survivor of rabies who did not take the vaccine.<ref name="n139">{{cite journal |last=McKenna |first=Maryn |title=Rethinking Rabies |journal=Scientific American |publisher=Scientific American, a division of Nature America, Inc. |volume=309 |issue=2 |year=2013 |issn=0036-8733 |jstor=26017888 |pages=28–31 |url=http://www.jstor.org/stable/26017888 |access-date=June 9, 2026}}</ref>{{rp|28}}

== Life after rabies ==
Giese returned to school for the 2005–2006 academic year after taking classes over the summer to catch up on missed work.<ref name="Hellpap">{{cite news |last1=Hellpap |first1=Andrew |title=Jeanna Giese celebrates her 16th birthday |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-reporter-jeanna-giese-celebrates-her/199289649/ |access-date=June 10, 2026 |work=[[The Reporter (Fond du Lac, Wisconsin)|The Reporter]] |date=June 6, 2005}}</ref> She could not play volleyball again while in high school.<ref name="v036" /> In 2005, she was an opener for the [[Milwaukee Bucks]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Johnson |first1=Mark |last2=Newson |first2=Kawanza |title=Rabies survivor adjusts to spotlight |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-des-moines-register-rabies-survivor/199290104/ |access-date=June 10, 2026 |work=[[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]] |agency=[[The Des Moines Register]] |date=December 4, 2005 |page=8A}}</ref> In 2006, Giese won the "Youth of the Year Award" given by the Fond du Lac Country Law Enforcement Association.<ref>{{cite news |title=Jeanna Giese wins Youth of the Year award |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-reporter-fondy-bat-bite-jan-20-2006/169258120/ |access-date=June 10, 2026 |work=[[The Reporter (Fond du Lac, Wisconsin)|The Reporter]] |date=January 20, 2006 |page=A3}}</ref>

She studied at [[Marian University (Wisconsin)|Marian University]] and transferred to [[Lakeland University]] in 2010, where she graduated in 2011 with a major in biology.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Johnson |first1=Mark |title=Rabies survivor Jeanna Giese graduates from college |url=https://archive.jsonline.com/news/wisconsin/121479779.html |access-date=June 9, 2026 |work=[[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]] |date=May 8, 2011 |archive-date=March 28, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260328085843/https://archive.jsonline.com/news/wisconsin/121479779.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

In August 2013, Giese's [[Siberian Huskies]] bit a rabies-infected bat. The dogs were vaccinated and quarantined.<ref name="Munoz" />

Giese has become an activist and [[public speaker]] for rabies information,<ref name="Razner" /> and was an ambassador for the [[Global Alliance for Rabies Control]] as of 2016.<ref name="experience">{{cite news |last1=Giese |first1=Jeanna |title=Experience: I was bitten by a rabid bat |url=https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2016/aug/26/experience-bitten-by-rabid-bat |access-date=June 9, 2026 |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=August 26, 2016}}</ref> On the first anniversary of Giese's rabies diagnosis, she attended and was the guest of honor at a meeting for rabies researchers, where she gave a speech.<ref name="d764" />{{rp|95}}

As of 2016, although Giese gained all motor ability back, her left side of the body reacts slower than her right and she has to perform regular exercise.<ref name="experience" />

Giese got married in 2014.<ref name="Munoz">{{cite news |last1=Munoz |first1=Carlos |title=FDL rabies survivor Jeanna Giese marries |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-sheboygan-press-fdl-rabies-survivor/199281957/ |access-date=June 10, 2026 |work=[[The Sheboygan Press]] |date=September 23, 2014 |page=A6}}</ref> {{As of|2024}}, she has three children and lives in Wisconsin, where she works at the [[Children's Museum of Fond du Lac]].<ref name="Elliott" /><ref>{{cite news |title=Jeanna Giese 16-years later: Surviving rabies to build a beautiful life |url=https://www.nbc26.com/news/local-news/jeanna-giese-16-years-later-surviving-rabies-to-build-a-beautiful-life |access-date=June 9, 2026 |work=[[WGBA-TV]] |date=July 20, 2021 |archive-date=June 25, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250625073755/https://www.nbc26.com/news/local-news/jeanna-giese-16-years-later-surviving-rabies-to-build-a-beautiful-life |url-status=live }}</ref>

